## DESCRIPTION
## Algebra, Exponential functions
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Linear equations and functions)
## DBsection(Linear functions)
## Date(06/16/2017)
## Institution(Colorado Community College System)
## Author(James Morski)
## Level(1)
## Static(1)
## MO(1)
## KEYWORDS('algebra','exponential functions')

#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

#  Setup

@x = (1 .. 4);
$a = random(5, 25);
$f = Compute("$a x")->reduce;
@y = map { $f->eval(x => $_) } @x;

$popup = DropDown([ "linear", "exponential", "neither" ], "linear");

BEGIN_PGML
Determine whether the table could represent a function that is linear, exponential, or neither.  If the function is exponential or linear, find a function that passes through the points.  If the function is neither, type *NONE*.

[#
    [.[`x`].] [.[$x[0]].][.[$x[1]].][.[$x[2]].][.[$x[3]].]*
    [.[`f(x)`].] [.[$y[0]].][.[$y[1]].][.[$y[2]].][.[$y[3]].]
#]{horizontalrules =>1, align => '|c|c|c|c|c|', padding => 0.3}


The function is [_]{$popup} 

An form of the function is [` f(x)= `] [_]{$f}{20} 

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Notice that in this case, each increase in [`x`], increases the [`y`]-value by [$a].  So the function has the form [`f(x) = [$a] x + b`] and therefore is linear. 

To find [`b`], solve 
[``` f(1) = 9 = [$a] x + b ```]
resulting in [`b=0`], so the function is [`f(x) = [$a]x `].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
